WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:06.620
(electronic music)

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.780
Hey there Internet People, Jared here.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.190
It’s been years since the DC unveiled their
expanded cinematic universe, but unfortunately,

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:17.780
these days, we can’t think of Batman or
Superman without getting a little bit sad.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.640
Or, very, very sad.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.400
(Hello Darkness my old friend)

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.880
- So today we’re gonna face that sadness,
together, and take a look at the big question

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.730
to end all big questions: what the hell went
wrong with the DCEU?

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.879
Sure, you can point to standalone successes.

00:00:33.879 --> 00:00:35.270
People seem to like Aquaman.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:36.499
Wonder Woman was pretty good.

00:00:36.499 --> 00:00:41.140
But unlike Marvel, which managed to make over
20 movies with only a few stinkers,

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.870
(Sad Horn Sound)

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:47.590
- DC’s grand ambitions seem to have crashed
and burned.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.479
How could a sure-thing turn out so disappointing?

00:00:50.479 --> 00:00:55.359
The way we see it, the problems can be traced
back to two comics from 1986, and the mismanaged

00:00:55.359 --> 00:00:58.649
influence they have had on superhero culture
at large.

00:00:58.649 --> 00:00:59.819
Allow us to explain.

00:00:59.819 --> 00:01:03.209
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The DCEU:
What Went Wrong?

00:01:03.209 --> 00:01:06.950
And of course, spoilers ahead for all of the
films in the DCEU as well as slight spoilers

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.340
for some excellent comics.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:13.960
Let’s dive in.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.940
Now, of course, the DCEU isn’t “over”
per se.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.640
Parts of it are even getting rebooted with
definite articles, such as THE Suicide Squad

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.970
and a highly anticipated THE Batman.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.440
Still, at a time when superhero franchises
are the biggest movies in the world, characters

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.930
like Cyborg, Flash, and, most egregiously,
Superman have seemingly disappeared.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.850
To be fair, it’s not for lack of trying,
but there were fundamental issues with their

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.310
plans from the very beginning.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.630
To make sense of those issues, we first need
to understand Superman and Batman, and what

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:47.380
they represent: Two different versions of
pure wish-fulfillment.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.070
Not only in terms of putting on a cape and
fighting crime, which would be awesome, but

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.890
in terms of the way they make us think about
human potential.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:57.840
Superman, in all his godlike power, reflects
what we wish we could be.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.370
He’s, by definition, more than human.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:03.870
He is the fantasy of being freed from our
physical limitations.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.230
Batman, on the other hand, made himself who
he is.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:13.120
Through drive, will, persistence and a sizable
inheritance, he pushed himself to the absolute

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.800
extremes of human capability.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.200
In other words, he’s what we know we could
be if we applied ourselves and maybe had a

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.200
butler.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.460
The two heroes also differ greatly in their
psychology.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:28.620
Batman is irrevocably flawed; no matter how
hard he tries, or how many villains he punches,

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.160
he’ll never get over the tragic death of
his parents, and that’s led him on a personal

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:34.600
crusade against crime.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:39.040
He is constantly engaged in the struggle to
do the right thing because, essentially, there’s

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.900
not much separating him from the villains
he faces.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.690
The key way that he does distinguish himself
is in one clear, unbreakable rule; he will

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.420
not kill, lest he become like the mugger who
murdered his parents.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.420
Superman, for all his un-Earthly power, is
a rural farmboy at heart.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.160
He isn’t driven by trauma, he simply believes
that he has a moral and ethical duty to use

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.390
his power to help.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:04.240
He was raised to know that lying is wrong,
that you shouldn’t harm other people, and

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.570
that honest work is more than good enough.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.660
He doesn’t generally involve himself in
geo-politics or governmental policy because

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:12.370
he doesn’t see it as his place.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.480
He never uses more force than is necessary.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.710
At his core, he embodies everything good and
noble about the human spirit.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:23.140
Through this, he reinforces faith in our values
and provides hope.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.600
At least, these are your standard Batman and
Superman.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.650
Different artists have imagined Superman as
a communist or Batman as a vampire.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:35.590
But the way our two favorite superheroes manifested
in the DCEU, as helmed by Zach Snyder, can

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:39.600
directly trace their influence back to two
specific works that take a unique approach

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.410
to heroics: Alan Moore and Dave Gibbons’
“Watchmen”, and Frank Miller’s “The

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:44.760
Dark Knight Returns”.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.780
Both of these comics were revolutionary when
they debuted in 1986 because they subverted

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.920
and deconstructed so many superhero tropes,
by placing them within a “real world”

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:54.920
context.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.930
Both Watchmen and The Dark Knight Returns
pick apart the kind of society that would

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:02.090
necessitate superheroes and the kind of people
that would be drawn towards the superhero

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:03.090
life.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.870
They critique the very concepts of superheroics
and vigilantism, as well as the effects that

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:08.430
they would have on the world.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.190
Watchmen took a wide view of the genre as
a whole, using newly created characters to

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.310
represent the archetypes present in superhero
fiction.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:19.870
The Comedian, a stand in for flag wearing
patriotic heroes like Captain America, gleefully

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:25.310
quells social unrest and commits atrocities
in Vietnam, content to be a tool for the government.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:30.170
The all powerful Dr. Manhattan mirrors all
of the unimaginably powerful heroes like Superman,

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.120
Captain Atom, or Silver Surfer.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.210
But Manhattan’s ultimate power leads to
an increasing disconnect from the human condition

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.300
thereby questioning the fundamental humanity
of this type of superbeing, which tacitly

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.580
leads us to consider what connects characters
like Superman to people like us.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.710
And the kind of cold-logic and “greater
good” mentality that characters like Batman

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:54.150
so often fall in to also comes into question,
with Ozymandias’ Squid-based master plan

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:57.780
to unite the world under a common enemy by
killing millions.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.210
This brings the very notion of heroism itself
into question, pushing us to examine our own

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.550
moral concepts of justice and good.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:09.670
Gadget loving detectives, obsessive crusaders,
the legacy hero: all these archetypes and

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:14.491
more are expertly folded into a handful of
characters . However, as we’ve seen, Moore

00:06:14.491 --> 00:06:18.490
still treats them as real people with complex
psychology.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.410
None of them are truly good, or truly bad.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:26.130
Whereas Watchmen looks at the entire genre,
The Dark Knight Returns hones in on a single

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.550
figure: Batman.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:32.150
It imagines a much-older Caped Crusader who
has aged in real-time, eschewing the then

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:35.350
unchallenged convention of un-aging comic
book heroes.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:39.730
We see the Batman/Robin dynamic in a new light
too with Batman characterizing the series

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:42.440
of Robin’s that have served with him as
“soldiers”.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:47.100
In this telling, the death of the second Robin,
Jason Todd, was the biggest factor in Batman’s

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.290
decision to retire.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:52.611
But when he fails to quell his crime-fighting
urges and dons the cape and cowl again, he

00:06:52.611 --> 00:06:56.330
is more than willing to take another impressionable
child under his wing.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:00.900
This directly addresses the inherent irresponsibility
of Batman in regards to enlisting children

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.000
and teenagers into his hyper-violent war on
crime.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.900
Miller also re-appraises the mythic, perpetual
struggle between Batman and The Joker.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:12.460
Here, The Joker has been pretty much comatose
since Batman retired, confined to Arkham and

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:13.889
seemingly compliant.

00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:18.669
But after Batman returns to Gotham, The Joker
too resurfaces to continue their feud. Miller

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:22.990
paints their conflict as an effect of Batman’s
presence, suggesting that maybe it was Batman

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:27.730
himself who caused crime to evolve into super-crime
and the stakes to be irreversibly raised in

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:28.730
Gotham City.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:32.840
Miller also features Superman as a naive,
ignorant yes-man for the government, who wages

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:37.170
a climactic battle against Batman essentially
“because the President said so”.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:41.669
Overall, Watchmen and The Dark Knight Returns
broke new ground in the development of superheroes,

00:07:41.669 --> 00:07:44.740
and were heralded for their self-aware critiques
of the genre.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:49.979
But as revolutionary as they were, both books
come to somewhat pessimistic conclusions about

00:07:49.979 --> 00:07:50.979
superheroes.

00:07:50.979 --> 00:07:54.229
Put simply, they’re grim, grimy, and hella
cynical.

00:07:54.229 --> 00:07:58.740
They both feature the governmental regulation
and prohibition of superheroes, for example,

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:03.260
suggesting that the natural response to superbeings
would be military application and they both

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:08.560
paint superheroes as psychologically compromised,
driven by neuroses and disorders.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.680
Unfortunately, the comics industry didn’t
really get this.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.970
After the massive critical and commercial
success of Watchmen and The Dark Knight Returns,

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:20.910
everyone tripped over themselves to mimic
the grim, dark aesthetic of these two revolutionary

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:21.910
works.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:25.490
But their imitation was only surface level
- , and led to what is known as The Dark Age

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:26.490
of Comics.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:31.110
Suddenly everything had to be dark and gritty,
and superheroes turned into a**holes who killed

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.820
villains indiscriminately.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.539
Optimism was seen to be cheesy, replaced with
baseless angst.

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:41.860
Blood, gore and rampant oversexualization
were everywhere, but seemingly just for the

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:42.860
aesthetic.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:47.079
Without the nuance and complexity of Watchmen
and The Dark Knight Returns, the stories were

00:08:47.079 --> 00:08:48.580
ultimately hollow.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:49.920
Enter Zack Snyder.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.199
Before the DCEU, Snyder was becoming known
as the “comic book guy” in Hollywood,

00:08:54.199 --> 00:08:56.680
mostly due to his faithful adaptation of Watchmen.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:03.170
And during press for Watchmen, Snyder lauded
two comics over all others; Watchmen (duh),

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:07.889
and The Dark Knight Returns, even mentioning
his desire to adapt the latter for film.

00:09:07.889 --> 00:09:12.529
And here’s where we get into the real meat
as to why the DCEU fell flat on its face.

00:09:12.529 --> 00:09:16.790
The same cynical deconstruction that worked
for Watchmen and The Dark Knight Returns seem

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:20.149
to inform every decision Synder made when
building the DCEU.

00:09:20.149 --> 00:09:24.959
He could only see superheroes and the world
they inhabit through a grimy, pessimistic

00:09:24.959 --> 00:09:25.959
lens.

00:09:25.959 --> 00:09:30.300
But just like the Dark Age of Comics, Snyder
seemed preoccupied with the dark surface,

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:31.990
rather than the intellectual core.

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:36.420
Furthering this, his overriding worship of
those fabled deconstructivist comics caused

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.930
big problems when he drew from other, less
gritty, source material.

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:43.649
To highlight this, let’s look at Man of
Steel, the origin story for the granddaddy

00:09:43.649 --> 00:09:44.920
of all superheroes.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:49.100
Snyder actually draws from a lot of influences
here, predominantly the classic, optimistic

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.920
Superman comics Birthright by Mark Waid, and
All-Star Superman by Grant Morrison.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:58.149
The movie wears these influences on its sleeve;
things like Clark flying alongside a herd

00:09:58.149 --> 00:10:00.930
of zebra and the S Crest on his costume standing
for hope

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:02.910
- That’s what this symbol means.

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:06.049
The symbol of the House of El means hope.

00:10:06.049 --> 00:10:07.259
- Are pulled straight from Birthright.

00:10:07.259 --> 00:10:10.609
He even lifts this speech almost word for
word from All-Star Superman.

00:10:10.609 --> 00:10:15.569
- You will give the people of Earth an ideal
to strive towards.

00:10:15.569 --> 00:10:17.809
They’ll race behind you.

00:10:17.809 --> 00:10:21.809
They will stumble they will fall.

00:10:21.809 --> 00:10:27.029
But in time… they will join you in the sun
Kal.

00:10:27.029 --> 00:10:29.089
- But Snyder’s nods to these inspirations
are, essentially, lipservice.

00:10:29.089 --> 00:10:30.669
- It’s not an S. On my world it means hope.

00:10:30.669 --> 00:10:39.220
- We get acknowledgements by way of shared
designs, imagery and dialogue, but Snyder

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:43.569
seemingly can’t reconcile their hopeful,
uplifting themes with his own cynicism.

00:10:43.569 --> 00:10:47.740
We know this because, whenever possible, Man
of Steel takes the dark and gritty option.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:51.399
We covered this in our What Went Wrong for
Man of Steel, but between the rampant collateral

00:10:51.399 --> 00:10:56.139
damage, and Superman actively murdering Zod
at the climax, Man of Steel doesn’t look

00:10:56.139 --> 00:10:58.310
kindly upon The Big Blue Boy Scout.

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:03.139
The creative team has explained that the intention
was to have him eventually grow into the morally-grounded

00:11:03.139 --> 00:11:04.970
Superman we know and love.

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:09.860
But here’s the problem: Snyder introduces
the character already subverted, his angst

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:14.579
and general hand-wringing placing him closer
to a reluctant hero instead of the paragon

00:11:14.579 --> 00:11:16.370
of truth, justice and hope.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.319
But Superman is arguably the archetypical
superhero.

00:11:19.319 --> 00:11:24.089
You can’t introduce a definitive archetype
and deconstruct that archetype simultaneously.

00:11:24.089 --> 00:11:27.750
Otherwise you’re not introducing Superman,
you’re introducing Big Flying Dude.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:32.589
The proto-Superman that we see in Man of Steel
is, ultimately, so far from the Superman that

00:11:32.589 --> 00:11:35.850
we recognize that he’s a different character
entirely.

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:39.450
Snyder gave us even more insight into this
choice in an interview, in which the journalist

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:44.009
stated that “the so-called No-Win scenario
is the only way to test a hero’s rules and

00:11:44.009 --> 00:11:45.009
ethics”.

00:11:45.009 --> 00:11:48.670
Snyder agreed, adding that “...it’s the
only way to move forward with a hero, because

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:51.429
otherwise the hero drowns in the mire of his
own morality”.

00:11:51.429 --> 00:11:55.550
According to this, you can view the climax
of Man of Steel as the ultimate moral and

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:56.920
ethical test.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.139
Except Superman fails.

00:11:59.139 --> 00:12:01.329
In every conceivable way.

00:12:01.329 --> 00:12:05.959
Literally the first meta-human Superman has
to deal with causes him to lose all focus,

00:12:05.959 --> 00:12:10.920
allowing Metropolis to be leveled, countless
people to die, and Superman becomes a murderer.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.029
Snyder’s Superman doesn’t even seem to
register that he could do anything else other

00:12:14.029 --> 00:12:15.649
than snap some necks.

00:12:15.649 --> 00:12:20.980
This is a textbook example of “dark and
gritty” disguised as supposed clever subversion

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:24.660
It’s worth stating that, at this point,
Snyder’s plan to essentially build up to

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.790
a “true” Superman was still viable.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:28.839
We had only seen one movie, after all.

00:12:28.839 --> 00:12:32.689
Which brings us to Batman v Superman, and
where the cracks really begin to show.

00:12:32.689 --> 00:12:37.089
See, Batman v Superman was supposed to be
another Superman film, a direct sequel to

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:41.199
Man of Steel, but then Snyder decided to add
Batman in there and make Miller’s The Dark

00:12:41.199 --> 00:12:43.480
Knight Returns the central influence.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:47.880
Batman v Superman is notorious for being a
dumpster fire of a movie, and again, we’ve

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:49.779
covered it in its own What Went Wrong.

00:12:49.779 --> 00:12:54.439
But for this video, let’s look at Snyder’s
two main inspirations: The Dark Knight Returns,

00:12:54.439 --> 00:12:56.369
as mentioned, and The Death of Superman.

00:12:56.369 --> 00:13:00.989
What Snyder chooses to take from them, and
what he chooses to omit, once again demonstrate

00:13:00.989 --> 00:13:04.480
his fascination with surface level, gritty
subversion.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:09.220
The first time we see them together on the
big-screen, we get a frankly psychotic Batman

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:14.560
killing goons, branding people, and straight-up
pre-meditating the cold blooded murder of

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:15.560
Superman.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:16.560
And Superman?

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:20.300
Well he’s just sort of there, perhaps, presumably,
internally struggling.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:24.319
In taking the surface level angst and violence
from The Dark Knight Returns, but ignoring

00:13:24.319 --> 00:13:28.009
the deeper themes, Snyder creates a contradiction
in character.

00:13:28.009 --> 00:13:31.949
Whereas The Dark Knight Returns asks us to
consider the archetypal Batman’s relationship

00:13:31.949 --> 00:13:36.499
to violence or Robin, Snyder simply wants
us to consider how cool it is that Batman

00:13:36.499 --> 00:13:37.720
brands people.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:42.410
Or put another way, The Dark Knight Returns
is in conversation with, and criticizes, the

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:43.999
established Batman mythos.

00:13:43.999 --> 00:13:46.839
Batman v Superman, on the other hand, is talking
to itself.

00:13:46.839 --> 00:13:49.610
And it’s mostly saying: “grit is good”

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:53.769
The same issues apply to Snyder’s use of
The Death of Superman - which ironically could

00:13:53.769 --> 00:13:57.240
have helped Snyder build up his wholesome
version of the man of steel.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:01.100
Released in the midst of the Dark Age of Comics,
The Death of Superman, sees Superman die at

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:02.569
the hands of Doomsday.

00:14:02.569 --> 00:14:07.910
Four separate heroes, Superboy, Steel, Eradicator
and Cyborg Superman, try to fill the hole

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:11.929
that his death has left in Metropolis, to
varying degrees of success.

00:14:11.929 --> 00:14:16.110
But when a bigger threat in the form of Mongul,
aided by none other than Cyborg Superman,

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:21.209
appears to wreak havoc, Superman returns from
the “dead”, proving to be the only one

00:14:21.209 --> 00:14:24.790
who can stop them, and be worthy of the title
of Superman.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:28.549
Thematically, the whole story revolves around
the notion that a dark and gritty Superman

00:14:28.549 --> 00:14:32.989
isn’t Superman at all-- the methods of the
4 replacement “Supermen” actually reflect

00:14:32.989 --> 00:14:35.060
the edgy, grim heroes of the ‘90s.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:39.999
However, the only thing Snyder takes away
from this is: Superman + death.

00:14:39.999 --> 00:14:44.170
Rather than seeing how Superman’s death
could fulfill his arc of redeeming him, Snyder

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:50.980
ignores everything else to, once again, hyper-focus
on the surface fact that Superman done died.

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:54.350
By the time Justice League rolled around,
the damage had already been done.

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:58.920
We pick up where Synder left off: Superman’s
dead, Batman’s guilt-ridden and struggling

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:02.639
with his violent tendencies and Wonder Woman’s
oddly detached from everything.

00:15:02.639 --> 00:15:08.009
While we may never know the entirety of Snyder’s
plans, what we do know paints a somewhat conflicted

00:15:08.009 --> 00:15:09.009
picture.

00:15:09.009 --> 00:15:12.749
On the one hand, Snyder’s vision would have
revealed Martian Manhunter and Green Lantern

00:15:12.749 --> 00:15:17.369
completing the iconic Justice League lineup
facing off against Darkseid, which makes it

00:15:17.369 --> 00:15:19.749
hard to not pine for what could have been.

00:15:19.749 --> 00:15:23.459
But on the other hand, even more time would
have been spent in the Knightmare future,

00:15:23.459 --> 00:15:27.790
with a decimated Justice League fighting back
against a brain-washed fascist Superman.

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:31.579
That part would have been inspired by Tom
Taylor and Brian Buccellato’s Injustice

00:15:31.579 --> 00:15:36.589
comics, a grim, gritty, and cynical deconstructive
tale that asks: “What if Superman broke

00:15:36.589 --> 00:15:37.589
bad?”.

00:15:37.589 --> 00:15:41.069
Based on Synder’s previous adaptations,
it’s not hard to extrapolate: We suspect

00:15:41.069 --> 00:15:45.480
the same kind of surface level, cynical interpretation
would have come into effect here too.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:48.529
But ultimately, we can’t know for sure,
because Snyder didn’t get to complete his

00:15:48.529 --> 00:15:52.230
vision and we’ll never really know if his
controversial choices would have paid off.

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:56.709
Still, the fact remains that we did get most
of the story, and most of us hated it.

00:15:56.709 --> 00:16:00.360
So much so, that it killed the idea of an
interconnected cinematic universe dead

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:04.559
It’s not all doom and gloom though! DC and
Warner Bros have stepped back from their sprawling

00:16:04.559 --> 00:16:08.709
plans of connectivity, handing the reins over
to a creative teams that are willing to embrace

00:16:08.709 --> 00:16:11.859
the bright, playful, and hopeful nature of
the genre.

00:16:11.859 --> 00:16:16.999
Wonder Woman, Aquaman, and Shazam have generated
warmer reception from fans and critics alike,

00:16:16.999 --> 00:16:20.209
and indicate a less depressing future for
DC films.

00:16:20.209 --> 00:16:21.569
But what do you think, Wisecrack?

00:16:21.569 --> 00:16:23.420
Did Synder miss the point?

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:25.170
Or are we being too hard on the guy?

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:26.420
Let us know what you think in the comments.

00:16:26.420 --> 00:16:30.019
Thanks to our dope patrons for supporting
the channel and our podcasts.

00:16:30.019 --> 00:16:31.720
Slam that subscribe button.

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:51.769
Thanks for watching and, as always, peace.

